    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_9351c6debcb4935c1f62ef140a3b02900745567669544f8a90ecd4e3a7737cdf",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Under RH, Lemma 2 converts a short-interval prime-counting discrepancy into a squared-integral comparison with a weighted zero sum. The proof first invokes the preceding Lemma 1 comparison, then uses Cauchy-Schwarz to separate error terms; a uniform sieve inequality controls prime-pair contributions, while integrated mean-square estimates control the remaining pieces. Smoothing and squaring gain a modular energy bound in which different errors can be estimated independently, but lose pointwise information and yield only an upper-bound reduction, not an equality. The preceding comparison, the sieve input, RH, and the unrecoverable parameter restrictions remain essential proof burdens.",
