    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_b9f6082cda722d4fc0f2db691aef0e5ccf4445c292b8c680ac7422cdaf85b8d6",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A large consecutive-prime gap forces a nonnegative short-interval variance contribution, reducing gap control to a weighted zero-sum estimate. Splitting that estimate by ordinate then matches two different controls: the low-frequency part is handled directly by partial summation and pair-correlation energy, whereas the high-frequency weight is decomposed across truncation heights, producing a maximum over a family of pair-correlation scales. Thus the scale-uniform hypothesis is required by the high-frequency representation rather than by the gap encoding alone.",
